                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30222/2609.30222v1/deduplication_sliding_window.png",
                "caption": "Figure 5:Sliding-window tracking and cross-window de-duplication pipeline.Top row (Windowii):A temporal window of frames is processed by TrackEverything to predict dense 3D tracks across all points.\nAt the final timestep of the window, the predicted 3D point locations (here 46k points) are voxelized by quantizing coordinates into spatial voxels and mean-pooling co-located tokens. This cross-frame de-duplication prunes redundant surface observations (reducing point count from 46k to 16k).Bottom row (Windowi+1i+1):When the subsequent frame window arrives, its frames are lifted into a 3D point cloud using a feedforward geometry predictor (or sensor depths).\nThese newly observed points are concatenated with the de-duplicated points carried over from previous windows (serving as persistent 3D scene memory) to construct the active set of points to track.\nTrackEverything then produces dense 3D trajectories for all points across all timesteps of Windowi+1i+1.\nBy chaining sliding-window processing with boundary de-duplication, TrackEverything tracks extended video sequences while bounding active token counts to unique physical scene geometry rather than video duration.",
                "position": 1906
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30222/2609.30222v1/waft.png",
                "caption": "Figure 6:3D WAFT projection and iterative refinement dynamics.Top:Projections across time under zero-velocity initialization. Source points initialized at frameT=0T=0(red dots) are projected to future timesteps (T=7T=7andT=15T=15) using camera extrinsics and intrinsics. Red dots denote projected 2D locations under the zero-velocity prior (𝐱tgt(0)=𝐱src\\mathbf{x}_{\\mathrm{tgt}}^{(0)}=\\mathbf{x}_{\\mathrm{src}}in world space); green dots mark true ground-truth (GT) locations, with green arrows atT=15T=15indicating displacement error. For static objects, camera projection accounts for ego-motion and aligns projected points near GT; for dynamic objects, world motion leads to significant spatial offset.Bottom:Zoomed-in patches across refinement iterations (Iter∈{0,1,2}\\text{Iter}\\in\\{0,1,2\\}) atT=7T=7andT=15T=15.Static point (top):The zero-velocity initialization atIter=0\\text{Iter}=0already places the sampled point close to the true location. The bilinearly sampled target feature𝐠tgt\\mathbf{g}_{\\mathrm{tgt}}closely matches the source feature𝐠src\\mathbf{g}_{\\mathrm{src}}, signaling to the model that no large displacement is required and maintaining stability across iterations 1 and 2.Dynamic point (bottom):AtIter=0\\text{Iter}=0, the zero-velocity projection misses the moving object by multiple pixels (landing on the background), creating an appearance mismatch between𝐠src\\mathbf{g}_{\\mathrm{src}}and𝐠tgt\\mathbf{g}_{\\mathrm{tgt}}in the concatenated WAFT feature.\nDriven by this mismatch, the endpoint refiner iteratively updates 3D positions: byIter=1\\text{Iter}=1the point moves substantially closer, and byIter=2\\text{Iter}=2the sampled point locks onto the correct surface feature.",
                "position": 1969
            },
